{0 Bindings}

Bindings give a Worker access to values and Cloudflare resources through its
[env] argument. Configure variables and resources in [wrangler.toml], and set
secrets with Wrangler. Use {!Workero.Env.var} to read a string variable or
secret. Use the resource's [of_env] function to read a resource binding.

{1 Choose an API}

- {{!page-env}Variables and secrets}: read string configuration with
  {!Workero.Env.var}.
- {{!page-kv}KV}: store text by key with {!Workero.KV}.
- {{!page-r2}R2}: store buffered object bytes with {!Workero.R2}.
- {{!page-d1}D1}: run SQL with {!Workero.D1} and {!Workero.Sql_value}.

workero supports these bindings, not every binding type in the
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/bindings/}Cloudflare bindings API}.
The {{!page-cache}Cache API} is not a binding. {!Workero.Cache} uses the global
[caches.default] runtime API; see {{!page-runtime_apis}Runtime APIs}.

{1 Declare resource names}

For local development, Wrangler can create local resources from the binding
names alone:

{@toml[
[[kv_namespaces]]
binding = "KV"

[[r2_buckets]]
binding = "BUCKET"

[[d1_databases]]
binding = "DB"
]}

[npx wrangler dev] uses local storage by default. You do not need to create
remote resources to test these bindings locally. For a deploy, either let
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/wrangler/configuration/#automatic-provisioning}Wrangler provision resources}
from these names or create remote resources with an authenticated Wrangler and
add their IDs or names to [wrangler.toml]. Each resource guide shows both paths.

{1 Read a binding in a handler}

The handler receives [env]. {!Workero.Env.var} returns [Some value] only for a
string variable or secret; a missing or non-string value returns [None].
[KV.of_env env "KV"], [R2.of_env env "BUCKET"] and [D1.of_env env "DB"]
look up resource objects. They raise [Invalid_argument] with the binding name
when the name is missing, the value is not an object of the expected shape, or
the object lacks a required method. [of_env] does not perform a storage request.

Storage operations suspend like {!Workero.Promise.await}, so call them in
direct style inside the handler. Runtime errors raise {!Workero.Js_error};
{!Workero.Promise.error_to_string} formats the underlying JavaScript error.
